"""entities_tbl — GENERATED named-character-reference table for
strip_html.mojo.

Source: golang.org/x/net@v0.57.0 html/entity.go (`entity` + `entity2` maps,
2231 records merged) — the exact table bluemonday v1.0.27 resolves entities
through in alugue_rust_skinny_go. Regenerated by parsing the module-cache
entity.go and emitting fixed-width records; DO NOT hand-edit AND do not
regenerate casually: BYTE-PARITY LAW — every entity decision in
strip_html_to_text flows through these bytes, so any content change shows up
in HTTP responses. Docs-only edits are safe; anything else requires the full
golden-belt procedure (test_strip + DB fuzz diff vs Go).

Layout
    ENT_COUNT   = 2231 records, in STRICT byte order of the padded name so a
                  plain binary search works.
    ENT_NAMES   = ENT_COUNT * ENT_NAME_W (34) bytes: each record holds the
                  reference name left-justified and space-padded. Padding is
                  part of the sort key: ' ' sorts below every legal name
                  byte, which makes byte-compare against the PADDED form
                  equal map-order comparison (see _cmp_query_rec). Longest
                  name WITH semicolon is 32 bytes
                  ("CounterClockwiseContourIntegral;"), hence width 34.
    ENT_VALUES  = ENT_COUNT * 12 lowercase hex digits per record:
                  rune1(6) then rune2(6); rune2 == "000000" marks a
                  single-rune entity (the entity2 pairs like NotEqualTilde
                  carry both).
    LONGEST_WITHOUT_SEMI = 6 — length of the longest LEGACY no-semicolon
                  record ('Aacute', 'Ccedil', ...); 106 such bare records
                  exist ('amp', 'copy', 'not', 'times', ...). strip_html's
                  named-ref fallback uses it as the prefix-walk bound,
                  mirroring x/net unescapeEntity's min(len-1, longestWithoutSemi).
    ENT_REC     = 46 historical combined-record stride (34 name + 12 value);
                  nothing consumes it today, kept so regeneration tooling
                  stays describable against this header.

Consumer contract (strip_html.entity_lookup): exact-match lookup of one
name WITHOUT its leading '&', returning (rune1, rune2-or-0, found); the
semicolon-less prefix fallback lives on the CALLER side there.

origin: alugue-mojo-api utils/entities_tbl.mojo
"""

